    {
      "id": "calls[0]/steps[1].reasoning",
      "type": "thinking",
      "kind": "value",
      "label": "Thinking",
      "value": "Let me analyze the examples:\n\nAccepted:\n- AB-123\n- ZZ-000\n- QT-456-R\n- MN-789\n- XY-001-R\n\nRejected:\n- A-123 (only one letter before dash)\n- ABC-123 (three letters before dash)\n- AB-12 (two digits)\n- AB-1234 (four digits)\n- ab-123 (lowercase)\n- AB-123-X (single letter suffix X, but accepted has -R)\n- AB123 (no dash)\n- AB-123-R-R (double suffix)\n\nSo the rule seems: two uppercase letters, dash, three digits, optionally dash and letter R.\n\nWait, accepted: QT-456-R and XY-001-R have -R suffix. Rejected: AB-123-X (suffix X not R), AB-123-R-R (double suffix). So suffix must be exactly \"-R\" if present.\n\nSo pattern: [A-Z]{2}-[0-9]{3}(-R)?\n\nLet me verify: AB-123 matches. ZZ-000 matches. QT-456-R matches. MN-789 matches. XY-001-R matches. A-123 fails (needs 2 letters). ABC-123 fails. AB-12 fails. AB-1234 fails. ab-123 fails (lowercase). AB-123-X fails (suffix must be -R). AB123 fails (no dash). AB-123-R-R fails (only one suffix).\n\nThat seems right. Let me test with try_pattern.",
      "parent": "calls[0]"
    },
